## Decision 02 - Depth Fog And Half-Res Particle CBuffer Cleanup
Problem: Two small runtime full-screen passes in the Presentation domain still pushed pass constants through material mutation: `HectonNoirDepthFogFeature` used material color/vector setters, and `HectonHalfResParticlesFeature` used material float setters. These were not gameplay truth, but they still violate the global-CBuffer direction and create avoidable dirty material state.
Solution: Added `HectonNoirDepthFogGlobals` as a 64-byte constant buffer with four `float4` rows: shallow fog color, abyss fog color, params A, params B. Added `HectonHalfResParticlesGlobals` as a 16-byte constant buffer with composite strength, bilateral depth scale, active flag, and explicit padding. Both passes use persistent `GraphicsBuffer.Target.Constant` resources, `LockBufferForWrite`, cache comparisons, and `Shader.SetGlobalConstantBuffer`. Their shaders now use named HECTON CBuffers instead of `UnityPerMaterial` for these pass globals.
Rejected Alternatives: MaterialPropertyBlock was rejected because these are full-screen/pass-level globals. Keeping material setters because they were cached was rejected because dirty-frame material mutation is still the wrong ownership model. Rewriting every remaining Visor feature in one pass was rejected as compile-wall risk. Restoring deleted `CURRENT_BATCH.md` was rejected as an unrelated batch-management change.
Scalability potential: Low/MX350 gets cheaper fake-first post effects with fewer material state mutations. Middle/High/Ultra keep the same ABI and can spend saved CPU/submission hygiene on stronger fog/particles without adding gameplay simulation.
Hardware Impact: No profiler capture was run, so no hard microsecond claim. Static effect: removes four material property uploads from dirty depth-fog frames and two material property uploads from dirty half-res particle frames. Both CBuffer structs are cache-line small and 16-byte aligned.

